00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.500
THE BLACK MARKET:
$400 to a $1000. This is how much you can make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:10.080
per article by writing about banks, presidents,&nbsp;
and other notable figures on Wikipedia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:16.860
That’s why teachers told us not to trust what we&nbsp;
see on Wikipedia. After all, anyone can change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:23.520
it. But, have you actually ever seen someone edit&nbsp;
or change a Wikipedia article. I know I haven’t.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:29.460
But somehow, there’s a Wikipedia article about&nbsp;
virtually any topic that you can think whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:35.880
that be President Calvin Coolidge's pet raccoon or&nbsp;
fluid mechanics. In fact, according to a Wikipedia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:41.520
page, there’s a total of 58 million Wikipedia&nbsp;
pages. Traditionally, these articles are written&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:48.000
by super dedicated volunteers. People who strongly&nbsp;
believe in the free sharing of information like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:54.060
this guy. This guy, Steven Pruitt, has apparently&nbsp;
made nearly 3 million edits on Wikipedia and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.060 --> 00:01:00.540
written 35,000 original articles all for no&nbsp;
compensation. He says that the idea of making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:07.080
it all free fascinates him, but not all Wikipedia&nbsp;
writers are quite as noble. Here’s the thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:12.840
the influence of Wikipedia has grown tremendously&nbsp;
over the past 20 years. Nowadays, the information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:19.020
found on Wikipedia is often taken as gospel&nbsp;
because oftentimes you don’t even know that it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:24.960
from Wikipedia. Take voice assistants for example.&nbsp;
Whether your asking Alexa, Google, or Siri, much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:30.600
of the information you’re consuming is directly&nbsp;
from Wikipedia. The same thing is true for ChatGPT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:37.020
as well which has been trained using the entirety&nbsp;
of Wikipedia. In fact, companies use Wikipedia so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:41.220
much that Wikipedia’s parent organization,&nbsp;
Wikimedia, has actually started charging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:46.800
companies for using Wikipedia, and their first&nbsp;
customer was none other than Google themselves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:52.320
This in itself has raised concerns about how&nbsp;
these big tech companies can exercise control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:59.160
over Wikipedia, but more importantly, this has&nbsp;
added an indescribable amount of credibility to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:04.980
Wikipedia. All of a sudden, writing for Wikipedia&nbsp;
isn’t just a case of contributing to some online&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:11.460
forum. It’s a case of defining the truth as what&nbsp;
you write will be repeated by billions of voice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:18.300
assistants and generative AIs around the world. I&nbsp;
think you can see why this would be an extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:24.660
valuable resource to push a certain narrative.&nbsp;
Welcome to the black market of Wikipedia.

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:29.820
PUBLISHING AN ARTICLE:
Writing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:38.340
Wikipedia articles to push a certain narrative can&nbsp;
be super lucrative, but getting into the business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:43.620
isn’t all that easy. According to teachers,&nbsp;
publishing articles is something that just about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:51.000
any random joe can do but that’s completely false.&nbsp;
In fact, the process of publishing to Wikipedia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:57.000
is extremely hard and long, especially for new&nbsp;
accounts which is where probably you’ll have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:01.560
start. It turns out that Wikipedia is one of the&nbsp;
only services that we all use on a regular basis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:06.660
but don’t have an account for. Anyway, once you&nbsp;
create a new account, you’ll be greeted by this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:11.640
page which is basically a dashboard to track&nbsp;
your social credit on Wikipedia. As you can see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:17.040
I have none but this is where you’ll have to start&nbsp;
if you wanna get into publishing actual articles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:22.500
Wikipedia will suggest a bunch of articles for&nbsp;
you to make small edits to in terms of spelling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:28.020
grammar, and tone. You’ll have to edit atleast 10&nbsp;
pages before you unlock publishing functionality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:32.640
but if you don’t wanna seem sus, you likely&nbsp;
want to make hundreds if not thousands of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:37.620
edits before writing full on articles. Once you&nbsp;
build up this Wikipedia credit congratulations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:43.260
you can finally start writing your very own&nbsp;
articles and publish them into the ether. Wait,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:48.540
nevermind, it’s not that easy. Even after&nbsp;
you build up social credit on Wikipedia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:54.720
you can’t just publish articles. Or I guess you&nbsp;
can but it doesn’t actually get published when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.720 --> 00:04:01.320
you press publish. Instead, it gets added to a&nbsp;
massive cue of over 4000 articles waiting to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:06.360
published. It usually takes about 6 months to get&nbsp;
through this cue after which your article will go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:10.980
through a rather arduous verification process.&nbsp;
The number one factor that Wikipedia is looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:16.740
for when approving articles is notability. No&nbsp;
amount of fancy writing or editing will get you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:21.780
around this requirement. In fact, Wikipedia&nbsp;
deletes and rejects over 200 articles every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:26.880
single day because they lack notability. How do&nbsp;
they determine notability? Well, it comes down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:31.440
to how many independent articles already exist&nbsp;
regarding the subject. Corporate press releases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:37.200
and websites do not count as notability for&nbsp;
obvious reasons. Wikipedia is looking for atleast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:42.600
3 high quality sources that contain substantial&nbsp;
discussion about the topic. A simple mention is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:46.800
not sufficient. They’re also scanning for any&nbsp;
sort of evident bias or narrative that you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:51.360
trying to push. These rules are already pretty&nbsp;
stringent if you’re writing about an organization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:56.340
or event. But if you decide to write about a&nbsp;
person, the scrutiny increases several fold.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.340 --> 00:05:01.320
The reason being that Wikipedia wants to avoid&nbsp;
libel and slander which was notoriously common&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:06.480
back in the early days. In fact, at one point&nbsp;
in time, Wikipedia claimed that Tony Blair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:12.240
the former prime minister of the UK, worshiped&nbsp;
Mr. small moustache. Nowadays, this wouldn’t fly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:17.580
because each and every line about a living person&nbsp;
has to have a direct reference. If it doesn’t,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:23.040
it gets removed even if its accurate. And all of&nbsp;
that is just scratching the surface of Wikipedia’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:28.800
verification process but if you’re able to get&nbsp;
through the gauntlet, well now you’re in business.

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:39.060
PAID ARTICLES:
Unlike the process of publishing an article,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:44.160
finding people who are willing to pay for an&nbsp;
article isn’t that hard. For obvious reasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:48.600
companies and people of interest are incentivized&nbsp;
to write articles about themselves to increase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:53.700
their notoriety. But, this is something that most&nbsp;
people and organizations avoid doing themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:59.040
because of the repercussions. Writing articles&nbsp;
about yourself is strictly prohibited as that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:03.780
a clear conflict of interest. If Wikipedia’s&nbsp;
reviewers sense that a given article was written&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:08.580
by an affiliate, not only is the article taken&nbsp;
down but the topic is flagged making it even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:13.140
harder for future articles to be published&nbsp;
about the topic. This is why many companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:19.020
turn to a professional albeit shady service that&nbsp;
specializes in this art. Such businesses have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:24.300
team of Wikipedia editors with a bunch of social&nbsp;
credit to push articles that clients want. One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:29.760
such business is Wikipedia Thrive. Their pitch&nbsp;
on the main page is literally “Hire Certified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:35.880
Wikipedia Consultants and Claim the Fame”. There&nbsp;
are certain parts of the page that almost make the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:41.160
page seem legit. For example, they claim that&nbsp;
they do exhaustive research and utilize domain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:46.680
experts to get authentic information. But, just&nbsp;
below that you run into statements like this. Our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:52.200
certified Wiki experts have complete command on&nbsp;
the exhaustive wiki guidelines, so they can get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:57.840
approval on the first run. Now, why exactly would&nbsp;
a legitimate article be worried about getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:03.840
approval? Obviously no reason at all. The reality&nbsp;
is that the entire site is designed to convince&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:09.900
customers about why Wikipedia pages are so&nbsp;
important, even more important than Google and why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:14.700
clients should invest in creating a page that will&nbsp;
surely get approved. From an outside perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:20.340
this is obviously super sketchy but the thing to&nbsp;
keep in mind is that the people who are looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:25.560
for this service are indeed looking for something&nbsp;
sketchy so it works out perfectly. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:31.200
according to their stats, it looks like business&nbsp;
is booming. Wikipedia Thrive apparently has 15,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:37.740
edits, 6000 live profiles, and 50,000 satisfied&nbsp;
clients. Even if we assume that each client was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:44.100
only charged a $100, we’re talking about $5&nbsp;
million in revenue. But, that’s honestly low&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:49.620
balling it. Way back in 2015, a freelance writer&nbsp;
named Mike Wood claimed that clients pay him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:56.880
$400 to a $1000 per article. So, 8 years later,&nbsp;
that rate has likely only gone up. Even at $400,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:02.880
we’re talking about $20 million in revenue. At a&nbsp;
$1000, we’re talking about as much as $50 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:08.100
in revenue and that’s just this one business.&nbsp;
There’s dozens just like them. This one company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:14.580
called WikiExpertsInc for example is apparently&nbsp;
ranked as the #1 Wikipedians company. They claim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.580 --> 00:08:20.760
to have published 4900 articles for 3400 clients&nbsp;
over the past 10 years and their motto is “Your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:25.560
Global Fame Is Just One Call Away!” Some&nbsp;
other businesses include wiki counsellor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:30.720
wikinative, wikiconsultancy, wikiconsultant,&nbsp;
americanwikieditors, and Lumino just to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:36.900
name a few. Some are more sketchy than others but&nbsp;
none are as sketchy as a true white glove service.

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:39.720
BENDING REALITY:&nbsp;

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:50.640
Most of the businesses we’ve covered so far&nbsp;
are definitely sketchy but they're not full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:54.720
on propaganda machines. They’ll simply cherry&nbsp;
pick sources that support a given narrative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.720 --> 00:09:00.120
and write articles using these sources. But,&nbsp;
some more white glove services will just go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:05.940
ahead and create the sources themselves which&nbsp;
brings us into the world of paid media. Getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:11.640
paid media itself is not that hard. There are&nbsp;
legitimate ways to make sponsored posts and/or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:17.040
get press releases featured on sites like Forbes&nbsp;
and CNBC. But, the problem with these avenues is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:22.980
that Wikipedia doesn’t accept sponsored posts or&nbsp;
press releases as valid sources. So, ironically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.980 --> 00:09:28.260
getting a Wikipedia page is actually harder than&nbsp;
being featured in mainstream media. To get into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:33.240
Wikipedia, not only do you have to be featured&nbsp;
on such websites in a substantial manner but you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:38.700
have to be featured in a seemingly natural and&nbsp;
independent manner. This is where PR agencies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:44.220
like Spynn come into play. The way this works is&nbsp;
pretty simple. These companies basically have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:48.180
bunch of connections with writers who work&nbsp;
at these outlets and they pay them a fee to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.180 --> 00:09:53.640
cover certain topics. Spynn has connections with&nbsp;
the following outlets. Theoretically speaking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:59.760
the writers are the one who control what ends up&nbsp;
getting published but this really just comes down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:04.260
the ethics of the given writer. There’s obviously&nbsp;
gonna be writers who are willing to post anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:10.380
for the right price. At Spynn for example you can&nbsp;
get you one story on Yahoo Finance or Business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:15.720
Insider for a $1000. They’re so confident in&nbsp;
their abilities that they even offer a money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:21.840
back guarantee. For $3000 a month, they can get&nbsp;
you into 6 publications within 3 months, so it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:27.240
really $9000 in total. And finally, for $5000&nbsp;
a month, they can get you into 12 publications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:34.320
within 3 months, so for $15,000 in total. Let’s&nbsp;
just say that this business is also booming as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:39.600
Spynn has been able to generate $30 million worth&nbsp;
of revenue within just 2 years of launching. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:45.420
looks like there’s a lot of egomaniacs out there&nbsp;
desperate for publicity and attention. Anyway, if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:50.160
you’re looking to get into Wikipedia from nothing,&nbsp;
you probably wanna opt for the enterprise package,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:55.140
so we’re talking about 15 grand. After that,&nbsp;
you’ll also have to pay for a thousand or so for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.140 --> 00:11:00.660
the Wikipedia article itself. So, we’re talking&nbsp;
about a total of 16 grand. That’s the price to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.660 --> 00:11:06.360
get into Wikipedia without any prior notoriety.&nbsp;
But, just because you get your wiki article posted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:10.980
doesn’t mean that you’re safe. Once an article&nbsp;
is posted, Wikipedia will continue to monitor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.980 --> 00:11:14.760
the interest on your page and other writers will&nbsp;
be able to suggest changes to the page. If your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:20.160
page doesn’t get much interest and no one suggests&nbsp;
any changes, that in itself will be a red flag.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:25.560
After all, what type of person who’s featured in&nbsp;
12 publications within a matter of 3 months gets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:31.320
only 7 views on their Wikipedia page. No man&nbsp;
at all. Wikipedia bots are constantly on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:37.080
look out for such pages. In fact, they take&nbsp;
down 1000 such pages on a daily basis. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.080 --> 00:11:41.760
if you wanna keep your Wikipedia page up, you’ll&nbsp;
have to start paying for bots to view your page.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:47.100
But, that’s simply the price you have to pay if&nbsp;
you’re trying to buy your way to fame as that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:52.320
the black market of Wikipedia. Someone else that’s&nbsp;
devoted to the free sharing of information is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:56.640
the founders of the Pirate Bay. If you wanna hear&nbsp;
about their legendary story, check out this video.

